# In-house Sepolia drip faucet environment — copy to deploy/faucet.env and
# fill in. `deploy/faucet.env` is gitignored; never commit a real key.
#
# HARD RULE: the key here is a HOT key on an internet-facing host. Fund it with
# a small float (the scout report's policy: <= 5 ETH hot at any time, the rest
# in a cold wallet) and top it up when /health says so. Even a total compromise
# of this host then loses the float, not the reserve.
#
# Generate a fresh throwaway key:
#   cargo run --release -p eth-faucet -- --genkey
# It prints the key and its address to STDOUT and writes nothing to disk.

# --- Key and chain (required) ---
# 64 hex chars, with or without 0x. THROWAWAY ONLY — never a personal or maker
# key. The maker keys next door are a separate rule for a separate reason
# (see maker.env.example); the reason here is that this one is reachable from
# the internet by design.
FAUCET_PRIVATE_KEY=REPLACE_ME_64_HEX_CHARS

# HTTP(S) only — the faucet sends transactions and reads balances, both plain
# JSON-RPC. A ws:// URL is refused at startup rather than failing later.
#
# The pinned public endpoint has been observed dropping eth_getLogs results
# silently in this project. The faucet never queries logs (balances and
# receipts only), so it is unaffected — but a dedicated RPC key is still worth
# it once the faucet is doing real volume.
FAUCET_RPC_URL=https://ethereum-sepolia-rpc.publicnode.com

# --- Where the rate-limit journal lives (already correct in the image) ---
# NOT optional in deployment: without it every address and IP cooldown resets
# on restart, and the limits stop being limits.
FAUCET_STATE_FILE=/app/state/faucet-ledger.json

# --- Drip size and limits (all optional; defaults shown) ---
# Every value is decimal ETH, parsed exactly (no floats).

# What one successful claim sends. ~4x the "~0.005 ETH covers a peer's gas"
# figure in docs/testnet.md, so a funded user gets several swaps out of one
# claim rather than coming straight back.
#FAUCET_DRIP_ETH=0.02

# Minimum gap between two drips to the same address.
#FAUCET_ADDRESS_COOLDOWN_SECS=86400

# Minimum gap between two drips to the same client IP. A SECONDARY signal only
# — VPNs are free and app users may share a NAT — which is why it is far
# shorter than the address cooldown. Set to 0 to disable it entirely (correct
# if this sits behind something that does not forward the client IP).
#FAUCET_IP_COOLDOWN_SECS=3600

# Total this faucet will ever send to one address, across all time.
#FAUCET_LIFETIME_CAP_ETH=0.1

# THE BACKSTOP. A hard stop per UTC day across every address: no combination of
# addresses, IPs, or solved puzzles can spend more than this in a day. Every
# other control just makes honest use cheap relative to abuse; this one bounds
# the worst case regardless. At the default 0.02 drip that is 50 claims/day.
#FAUCET_DAILY_BUDGET_ETH=1

# Refuse addresses that already hold at least this much — a UX refusal ("you
# already have gas"), not a sybil defense (a sybil just uses empty addresses).
# Set to 0 to disable.
#FAUCET_MAX_RECIPIENT_BALANCE_ETH=0.02

# --- Proof of work ---
# Difficulty in leading ZERO BITS of SHA256(seed || solution). 24 bits is ~16.7
# million hashes: a few seconds on one core. Each extra bit DOUBLES the work —
# 26 is ~4x this, 27 is ~8x — so raise it a bit at a time if abuse shows up.
# Refuses to start above 32.
#FAUCET_POW_DIFFICULTY_BITS=24

# How long an issued puzzle stays claimable. Long enough for a slow machine's
# solve, short enough that a farmed puzzle is worthless.
#FAUCET_CHALLENGE_TTL_SECS=300

# --- Server ---
#FAUCET_BIND=0.0.0.0:8787

# --- Logging ---
RUST_LOG=info
